</span></div>
</span></div>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">NLTVG in long videos.<span id="S4.T3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Our zero-shot grounding algorithm equipped with ResidualViT features outperforms the previous art both in accuracy and computational cost on the challenging long-form MAD dataset.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NLTVG in Long Videos.</span> Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Benchmarking on Downstream Tasks ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents additional results on the challenging long-form video MAD dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>, contrasting our ResidualViT against the only zero-shot grounding baseline available, which is described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>. This existing zero-shot grounding algorithm employs a proposal-based approach, utilizing a multi-scale sliding window technique to generate potential video segment proposals. For each proposal, a single feature representation is computed by average pooling frame features whose temporal locations fall within the proposal’s span. Finally, cosine similarity is computed between each sentence feature representation and each proposal feature representation. In contrast, our grounding algorithm requires the number of similarity computations to be equal to the number of encoded frames, which significantly reduces the computational complexity. Specifically, the proposal-based method demands approximately <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="20\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times</annotation></semantics></math> more similarity computations compared to our zero-shot grounding approach (described in Section <a href="#S3a" title="3 Zero-shot Grounding Algorithm ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> of supplementary). In these experiments, ResidualViT was configured with <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>, a token dropping probability <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}85\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and the center token dropping strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our results demonstrate that the grounding algorithm combined with ResidualViT visual features significantly outperforms the existing state-of-the-art (row 1 vs row 3 of Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Benchmarking on Downstream Tasks ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). When using the same backbone (ViT B/32), our approach achieves relative improvements ranging from <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43\%</annotation></semantics></math> at IoU=0.1 to <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128\%</annotation></semantics></math> at IoU=0.5, while also being <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53\%</annotation></semantics></math> more efficient in feature extraction and requiring one order of magnitude fewer similarity computations. Additionally, accuracy consistently increases with the use of more computationally demanding backbones. For example, using the ViT B/16 backbone, our method achieves a <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>160</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160\%</annotation></semantics></math> increase in accuracy at IoU=0.5, despite a <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="73\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>73</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">73\%</annotation></semantics></math> higher feature extraction cost compared to the baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>. These findings highlight an excellent tradeoff between computational cost and improved accuracy. Section <a href="#S6.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 6 Additional Long-Form NLTVG Comparisons ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
of supplementary expands Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Benchmarking on Downstream Tasks ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with comparisons against fully supervised methods and additional metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Automatic Audio Description.</span>
We demonstrate ResidualViT’s generalization by using its visual representations in an additional long-video understanding task: Automatic Audio Description <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. This challenging task, similar to dense video captioning, requires generating textual descriptions of video moments that capture events and characters.</p>
</div>
